(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacology-i-exam-1-dermatology-medications-quiz"]={"title":"Dermatology Medications Quiz 1 — Pharmacology I Exam 1","category":"Pharmacology I","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which problem does the lecture specifically attach to topical neomycin?","choices":["It frequently causes sensitisation","It bleaches hair and clothing","It causes hypopigmentation","It is inactivated by benzoyl peroxide"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and the class can also accumulate systemically. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"Which body sites does the lecture name as more permeable to a topical drug?","choices":["Shins, forearms, dorsal hands and neck","Scrotum, face, axilla and scalp","Palms, soles, knees and elbows","Back, chest, abdomen and buttocks"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Regional variation in penetration is the first variable the lecture lists, and these four are its examples. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"Which retinoid does the lecture pair with Kaposi sarcoma?","choices":["Adapalene","Alitretinoin","Bexarotene","Tazarotene"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Bexarotene is the one paired with T-cell lymphoma. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"What does the lecture advise patients about washing in acne?","choices":["Wash as often as possible to remove surface oils","Wash often with a high pH soap","Avoid washing the affected area entirely while on treatment","Twice a day only, balancing cleanliness against drying"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and it adds that once rinsed off no active product remains on the skin. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Which systemic corticosteroid does the lecture explicitly exclude from causing drug-induced acne?","choices":["Prednisone","Dexamethasone","Methylprednisolone","Hydrocortisone"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture notes the eruption is not seen with hydrocortisone. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"For which form of acne does the lecture name a topical retinoid as first line?","choices":["Noninflammatory, comedonal acne","Nodular acne with scarring","Acne associated with adrenal hyperactivity","Drug-induced acne from systemic corticosteroids"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and for inflammatory acne it is often combined with other agents rather than used alone. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"What warning does the lecture attach to polymyxin B?","choices":["Not on open wounds in high doses; neuro and nephrotoxicity","Avoid in pregnancy; absorption through inflamed skin","Avoid it in children under eight because it stains teeth","Avoid it with benzoyl peroxide because it is inactivated"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Loss of the barrier is what turns a topical agent into a systemic exposure. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"Which patient does the lecture say should avoid topical tacrolimus or pimecrolimus?","choices":["A patient with a weakened immune system","A patient with a history of acne","A patient over the age of sixty","A patient using an emollient"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and the lecture pairs this with a possible cancer risk. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"How does bacitracin work, and what does the lecture say about its systemic toxicity?","choices":["A peptide antibiotic; no systemic toxicity","Binds transfer RNA; accumulates systemically","It interrupts the cytoplasmic membrane, and it risks nephrotoxicity","It inhibits protein synthesis, and it frequently causes sensitisation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, with allergic dermatitis noted only rarely. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"A patient asks about the ciclopirox nail lacquer for onychomycosis. What does the lecture say about how well it works?","choices":["It is less than twelve per cent effective","It is curative in the majority of patients","It works only on fingernails, not toenails","It is as effective as oral terbinafine"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and saying so up front is what keeps expectations honest for a months-long treatment. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"Why does the lecture say an azole is sometimes combined with a corticosteroid?","choices":["To make the preparation less irritating to the skin","For more rapid symptom relief","To prevent the development of resistance","To allow a shorter total course of therapy"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The steroid addresses symptoms while the antifungal addresses the organism. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"Which laboratory change does the lecture list among isotretinoin's adverse reactions?","choices":["Reduced white cell count","Increased serum lipids","Reduced serum calcium","Raised serum immunoglobulin E"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and it sits alongside retinoid dermatitis, photophobia, arthralgia, headaches, alopecia and brittle nails. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"What are the two proposed actions of azelaic acid in the lecture?","choices":["Converts to benzoic acid, with a comedolytic peeling effect","Inhibition of T cell activation and mast cell stabilisation","Antimicrobial, and blocks testosterone conversion","Inhibition of fungal cell wall formation and keratinolysis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and the lecture is explicit that the mechanism is not fully understood. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"Which two problems does the lecture attach to intralesional steroid injection of an inflammatory nodule?","choices":["Bleaching of hair and clothing","Photosensitivity and severe sunburn","Systemic absorption leading to adrenal suppression, and local tissue atrophy","Permanent hyperpigmentation and keloid formation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Both are consequences of injecting a steroid into the lesion. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"What does the lecture say about nystatin's absorption?","choices":["It is well absorbed orally and distributes widely","It is absorbed only through inflamed skin","It accumulates systemically with repeated use","It has no oral absorption"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, which is why it is used for cutaneous and mucosal candidal infection rather than systemically. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"A parent asks what to do at home for a child with atopic dermatitis. Which set matches the lecture's non-pharmacologic advice?","choices":["Hot baths to open the pores, followed by air drying","Frequent washing with a high pH soap","Hot baths, high pH soap, air drying, occlusive dressings","Lukewarm baths, lubricant after, short nails, no overheating"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and the lecture adds distracting the child, removing irritants and allergens, and maintaining hydration. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"A patient on imiquimod reports redness, swelling and erosions at the site. What does the lecture say about this?","choices":["Irritation in nearly all; inflammation parallels efficacy","It indicates an allergic reaction and the drug should be stopped","It is unusual and suggests secondary bacterial infection","The dose is too low; increase the frequency"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, so the reaction is expected and is not by itself a reason to stop. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"Which two properties does the lecture use to distinguish adapalene from tretinoin?","choices":["Adapalene is used only for psoriasis rather than acne","Adapalene must be applied only at night because it is photolabile","Adapalene is stable in sunlight and stable with benzoyl peroxide","Adapalene is a systemic agent and requires pregnancy testing"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and it also tends to be less irritating. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"Which interaction does the lecture describe between benzoyl peroxide and tretinoin?","choices":["Tretinoin prevents benzoyl peroxide from converting to benzoic acid","The two together cause systemic hypervitaminosis A","Benzoyl peroxide inactivates tretinoin","Benzoyl peroxide increases tretinoin absorption to toxic levels"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and it is why adapalene, which is stable with benzoyl peroxide, is highlighted separately. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"Which three actions does the lecture attribute to topical retinoic acid?","choices":["Converts to benzoic acid, bleaches pigment, cuts sebum","Inhibits fungal ergosterol, reduces scaling, and stimulates interferon","Corrects keratinization and reduces P. acnes and inflammation","Dissolves existing comedones, blocks androgen receptors, and thins the epidermis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and the lecture still notes the mechanism is not completely understood. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"An exacerbation warrants a medium to high potency corticosteroid. What does the lecture say to do next?","choices":["Continue it indefinitely to prevent relapse","Stop abruptly once the flare settles and use nothing further","Add an oral corticosteroid for the same duration","Use it for one to two weeks, then switch to a lower potency"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The step-down is the protocol rather than continuing at strength. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"What is mupirocin's mechanism in the lecture?","choices":["It prevents bacterial cell wall synthesis","It interrupts the bacterial cytoplasmic membrane","It inhibits fungal ergosterol production and so prevents cell wall synthesis","It binds bacterial transfer RNA, preventing protein synthesis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and the lecture notes it is not absorbed though it may irritate mucous membranes. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"How does the lecture split topical against systemic therapy in acne?","choices":["Topical for the face, systemic for the trunk","Topical for mild to moderate disease, systemic for moderate to severe","Topical for inflammatory disease, systemic for comedonal disease","Topical for adults, systemic for adolescents"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Severity is what moves a patient onto systemic therapy. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"Which conditions make up the atopic triad the lecture places atopic dermatitis within?","choices":["Urticaria and angio-oedema","Acne vulgaris and rosacea","Asthma and allergic rhino-conjunctivitis","Psoriasis and seborrhoeic dermatitis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and skin barrier dysfunction is what the lecture says plays the major role in the skin component. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"Which two agents does the lecture name as allylamines, and how do they work?","choices":["Ciclopirox and sertaconazole, which block precursor uptake","Naftifine and terbinafine, inhibiting ergosterol","Clotrimazole and miconazole, which inhibit fungal cytochrome P450","Nystatin and tolnaftate, binding membrane sterols"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, with local irritation as the adverse reaction. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"What does bacterial lipase do in the acne cascade the lecture describes?","choices":["Digests the sebaceous gland basement membrane","Hydrolyses triglycerides into free fatty acids","Cleaves keratin so the follicular plug loosens","Converts testosterone to dihydrotestosterone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and those free fatty acids feed the increase in keratinization and microcomedone formation. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Which vehicle does the lecture place at the most drying end of its range, and which at the least?","choices":["Powders are the most drying and lotions the least","Tinctures are the most drying and ointments the least","Ointments are the most drying and tinctures the least","Creams are the most drying and gels the least"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The list runs tinctures, wet dressings, lotions, gels, aerosols, powders, pastes, creams, foams, ointments. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"A patient's corticosteroid is not controlling a plaque. Based on the concentration gradient principle in the lecture, what does that principle predict?","choices":["Changing to a more drying vehicle will matter more than concentration","Only a change to a systemic agent will overcome it","Raising the concentration increases drug transfer and can overcome the resistance","Concentration has little effect once the stratum corneum is saturated"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture gives corticosteroid resistance overcome by increased concentration as its worked example. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"Why does the lecture say an oral corticosteroid course for atopic dermatitis needs a tapering dose?","choices":["To avoid precipitating drug-induced acne","To allow the topical steroid to be started safely","To reduce the risk of secondary infection","To prevent a flare-up"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The taper protects against rebound rather than against withdrawal symptoms alone. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"What does the lecture say about isotretinoin and pregnancy?","choices":["Contraindicated in pregnancy and breastfeeding; men too","Safe after the first trimester","It is contraindicated only in the first trimester","It requires no pregnancy precautions because it is applied topically"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and the iPledge programme exists around exactly this risk. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 27"}]};
